#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:9
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"From: Junio C Hamano <gitster@pobox.com>\n"
"Date: Wed, 21 Nov 2007 16:32:55 -0800\n"
"Subject: Addendum to \"MaintNotes\"\n"
"Abstract: Imagine that Git development is racing along as usual, when our "
"friendly\n"
" neighborhood maintainer is struck down by a wayward bus. Out of the\n"
" hordes of suckers (loyal developers), you have been tricked (chosen) to\n"
" step up as the new maintainer. This howto will show you \"how to\" do it.\n"
"Content-type: text/asciidoc\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:11
#, no-wrap
msgid "How to maintain Git"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:14
#, no-wrap
msgid "Activities"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:17
msgid "The maintainer's Git time is spent on three activities."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:19
msgid "Communication (45%)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:23
msgid ""
"Mailing list discussions on general design, fielding user questions, "
"diagnosing bug reports; reviewing, commenting on, suggesting alternatives "
"to, and rejecting patches."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:25
msgid "Integration (50%)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:30
msgid ""
"Applying new patches from the contributors while spotting and correcting "
"minor mistakes, shuffling the integration and testing branches, pushing the "
"results out, cutting the releases, and making announcements."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:32
msgid "Own development (5%)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:34
msgid "Scratching my own itch and sending proposed patch series out."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:36
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Policy"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:41
msgid ""
"The policy on Integration is informally mentioned in \"A Note from the "
"maintainer\" message, which is periodically posted to the mailing list after "
"each feature release is made:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:45
msgid ""
"Feature releases are numbered as vX.Y.0 and are meant to contain bugfixes "
"and enhancements in any area, including functionality, performance and "
"usability, without regression."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:49
msgid ""
"Maintenance releases are numbered as vX.Y.Z (0 < Z) and are meant to contain "
"only bugfixes for the corresponding vX.Y.0 feature release and earlier "
"maintenance releases vX.Y.W (W < Z)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:54
msgid ""
"The 'master' branch is used to prepare for the next feature release. In "
"other words, at some point, the tip of 'master' branch is tagged as "
"vX.(Y+1).0, when vX.Y.0 is the latest feature release."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:60
msgid ""
"'maint' branch is used to prepare for the next maintenance release.  After "
"the feature release vX.Y.0 is made, the tip of 'maint' branch is set to that "
"release, and bugfixes will accumulate on the branch, and at some point, the "
"tip of the branch is tagged with vX.Y.1, vX.Y.2, and so on."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:68
msgid ""
"'next' branch is used to publish changes (both enhancements and fixes) that "
"(1) have worthwhile goal, (2) are in a fairly good shape suitable for "
"everyday use, (3) but have not yet demonstrated to be regression free.  "
"Reviews from contributors on the mailing list help to make the "
"determination.  After a topic is merged to 'next', it is tested for at least "
"7 calendar days before getting merged to 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:73
msgid ""
"'seen' branch is used to publish other proposed changes that do not yet pass "
"the criteria set for 'next' (see above), but there is no promise that 'seen' "
"will contain everything.  A topic that had no reviewer reaction may not be "
"picked up."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:77
msgid ""
"A new topic will first get merged to 'seen', unless it is trivially correct "
"and clearly urgent, in which case it may be directly merged to 'next' or "
"even to 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:86
msgid ""
"If a topic that was picked up to 'seen' becomes and stays inactive for 3 "
"calendar weeks without having seen a clear consensus that it is good enough "
"to be moved to 'next', the topic may be discarded from 'seen'.  Interested "
"parties are still free to revive the topic.  For the purpose of this "
"guideline, the definition of being \"inactive\" is that nobody has discussed "
"the topic, no new iteration of the topic was posted, and no responses to the "
"review comments were given."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:92
msgid ""
"The tips of 'master' and 'maint' branches will not be rewound to allow "
"people to build their own customization on top of them.  Early in a new "
"development cycle, 'next' is rewound to the tip of 'master' once, but "
"otherwise it will not be rewound until the end of the cycle."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:96
msgid ""
"Usually 'master' contains all of 'maint' and 'next' contains all of "
"'master'.  'seen' contains all the topics merged to 'next', but is rebuilt "
"directly on 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:99
msgid ""
"The tip of 'master' is meant to be more stable than any tagged releases, and "
"the users are encouraged to follow it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:103
msgid ""
"The 'next' branch is where new action takes place, and the users are "
"encouraged to test it so that regressions and bugs are found before new "
"topics are merged to 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:110
msgid ""
"When a problem is found in a topic in 'next', the topic is marked not to be "
"merged to 'master'.  Follow-up patches are discussed on the mailing list and "
"applied to the topic after being reviewed and then the topic is merged "
"(again) to 'next'.  After going through the usual testing in 'next', the "
"entire (fixed) topic is merged to 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:116
msgid ""
"One release cycle for a feature release is expected to last for eight to ten "
"weeks.  A few \"release candidate\" releases are expected to be tagged about "
"a week apart before the final release, and a \"preview\" release is tagged "
"about a week before the first release candidate gets tagged."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:122
msgid ""
"After the preview release is tagged, topics that were well reviewed may be "
"merged to 'master' before spending the usual 7 calendar days in 'next', with "
"the expectation that any bugs in them can be caught and fixed in the release "
"candidates before the final release."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:129
msgid ""
"After the first release candidate is tagged, the contributors are strongly "
"encouraged to focus on finding and fixing new regressions introduced during "
"the cycle, over addressing old bugs and any new features.  Topics stop "
"getting merged down from 'next' to 'master', and new topics stop getting "
"merged to 'next'. Unless they are fixes to new regressions in the cycle, "
"that is."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:134
msgid ""
"Soon after a feature release is made, the tip of 'maint' gets fast-forwarded "
"to point at the release.  Topics that have been kept in 'next' are merged "
"down to 'master' and a new development cycle starts."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:139
msgid ""
"Note that before v1.9.0 release, the version numbers used to be structured "
"slightly differently.  vX.Y.Z were feature releases while vX.Y.Z.W were "
"maintenance releases for vX.Y.Z."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:147
msgid ""
"Because most of the lines of code in Git are written by individual "
"contributors, and contributions come in the form of e-mailed patches "
"published on the mailing list, the project maintains a mapping from "
"individual commits to the Message-Id of the e-mail that resulted in the "
"commit, to help tracking the origin of the changes. The notes in "
"\"refs/notes/amlog\" are used for this purpose, and are published along with "
"the broken-out branches to the maintainer's repository."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:149
#, no-wrap
msgid "A Typical Git Day"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:153
msgid ""
"A typical Git day for the maintainer implements the above policy by doing "
"the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:159
msgid ""
"Scan mailing list.  Respond with review comments, suggestions etc.  Kibitz.  "
"Collect potentially usable patches from the mailing list.  Patches about a "
"single topic go to one mailbox (I read my mail in Gnus, and type \\C-o to "
"save/append messages in files in mbox format)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:164
msgid ""
"Write his own patches to address issues raised on the list but nobody has "
"stepped up to solve.  Send it out just like other contributors do, and pick "
"them up just like patches from other contributors (see above)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:169
msgid ""
"Review the patches in the saved mailboxes.  Edit proposed log message for "
"typofixes and clarifications, and add Acks collected from the list.  Edit "
"patch to incorporate \"Oops, that should have been like this\" fixes from "
"the discussion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:172
msgid "Classify the collected patches and handle 'master' and 'maint' updates:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:174
msgid ""
"Obviously correct fixes that pertain to the tip of 'maint' are directly "
"applied to 'maint'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:176
msgid ""
"Obviously correct fixes that pertain to the tip of 'master' are directly "
"applied to 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:177
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:192
msgid "Other topics are not handled in this step."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:179
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:194
msgid "This step is done with \"git am\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:183
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:198
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" $ git checkout master    ;# or \"git checkout maint\"\n"
" $ git am -sc3 mailbox\n"
" $ make test\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:186
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:201
msgid "In practice, almost no patch directly goes to 'master' or 'maint'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:188
msgid "Obviously correct fixes that pertain to the tip of 'maint'"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:189
msgid "are directly applied to 'maint'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:190
msgid "Obviously correct fixes that pertain to the tip of 'master'"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:191
msgid "are directly applied to 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:205
msgid ""
"Applying the e-mailed patches using \"git am\" automatically records the "
"mappings from 'Message-Id' to the applied commit in the \"amlog\" "
"notes. Periodically check that this is working with \"git show -s "
"--notes=amlog $commit\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:211
msgid ""
"This mapping is maintained with the aid of the \"post-applypatch\" hook "
"found in the 'todo' branch. That hook should be installed before applying "
"patches. It is also helpful to carry forward any relevant amlog entries when "
"rebasing, so the following config may be useful:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:225
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      [notes]\n"
"\trewriteRef = refs/notes/amlog\n"
"----\t\n"
"+\n"
"Avoid \"cherry-pick\", as it does not propagate notes by design. Use\n"
"either \"git commit --amend\" or \"git rebase\" to make corrections to\n"
"an existing commit, even for a single-patch topic.\n"
"+\n"
"Make sure that a push refspec for 'refs/notes/amlog' is in the\n"
"remote configuration for publishing repositories. A few sample\n"
"configurations look like the following:\n"
"+\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:230
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      [remote \"github\"]\n"
"\turl = https://github.com/gitster/git\n"
"\tpushurl = github.com:gitster/git.git\n"
"\tmirror\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:240
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      [remote \"github2\"]\n"
"\turl = https://github.com/git/git\n"
"\tfetch = +refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/github2/*\n"
"\tpushurl = github.com:git/git.git\n"
"\tpush = refs/heads/maint:refs/heads/maint\n"
"\tpush = refs/heads/master:refs/heads/master\n"
"\tpush = refs/heads/next:refs/heads/next\n"
"\tpush = +refs/heads/seen:refs/heads/seen\n"
"\tpush = +refs/notes/amlog\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:272
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" * Review the last issue of \"What's cooking\" message, review the\n"
"   topics ready for merging (topic->master and topic->maint).  Use\n"
"   \"Meta/cook -w\" script (where Meta/ contains a checkout of the\n"
"   'todo' branch) to aid this step.\n"
"+\n"
"And perform the merge.  Use \"Meta/Reintegrate -e\" script (see\n"
"later) to aid this step.\n"
"+\n"
" $ Meta/cook -w last-issue-of-whats-cooking.mbox\n"
"+\n"
" $ git checkout master    ;# or \"git checkout maint\"\n"
" $ echo ai/topic | Meta/Reintegrate -e ;# \"git merge ai/topic\"\n"
" $ git log -p ORIG_HEAD.. ;# final review\n"
" $ git diff ORIG_HEAD..   ;# final review\n"
" $ make test              ;# final review\n"
"+\n"
"And perform the merge.  Use \"Meta/Reintegrate -e\" script (see\n"
"later) to aid this step.\n"
"+\n"
" $ Meta/cook -w last-issue-of-whats-cooking.mbox\n"
"+\n"
" $ git checkout master    ;# or \"git checkout maint\"\n"
" $ echo ai/topic | Meta/Reintegrate -e ;# \"git merge ai/topic\"\n"
" $ git log -p ORIG_HEAD.. ;# final review\n"
" $ git diff ORIG_HEAD..   ;# final review\n"
" $ make test              ;# final review\n"
"+\n"
"If the tip of 'master' is updated, also generate the preformatted\n"
"documentation and push the out result to git-htmldocs and\n"
"git-manpages repositories.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:329
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" * Handle the remaining patches:\n"
" ** Anything unobvious that is applicable to 'master' (in other\n"
"    words, does not depend on anything that is still in 'next'\n"
"    and not in 'master') is applied to a new topic branch that\n"
"    is forked from the tip of 'master' (or the last feature release,\n"
"    which is a bit older than 'master').  This includes both\n"
"    enhancements and unobvious fixes to 'master'.  A topic\n"
"    branch is named as ai/topic where \"ai\" is two-letter string\n"
"    named after author's initial and \"topic\" is a descriptive name\n"
"    of the topic (in other words, \"what's the series is about\").\n"
" ** An unobvious fix meant for 'maint' is applied to a new\n"
"    topic branch that is forked from the tip of 'maint' (or the\n"
"    oldest and still relevant maintenance branch).  The\n"
"    topic may be named as ai/maint-topic.\n"
" ** Changes that pertain to an existing topic are applied to\n"
"    the branch, but:\n"
" *** obviously correct ones are applied first;\n"
" *** questionable ones are discarded or applied to near the tip;\n"
" ** Replacement patches to an existing topic are accepted only\n"
"    for commits not in 'next'.\n"
"+\n"
"The initial round is done with:\n"
"+\n"
" $ git checkout ai/topic ;# or \"git checkout -b ai/topic master\"\n"
" $ git am -sc3 --whitespace=warn mailbox\n"
"+\n"
"and replacing an existing topic with subsequent round is done with:\n"
"+\n"
" $ git checkout master...ai/topic ;# try to reapply to the same base\n"
" $ git am -sc3 --whitespace=warn mailbox\n"
"+\n"
"to prepare the new round on a detached HEAD, and then\n"
"+\n"
" $ git range-diff @{-1}...\n"
" $ git diff @{-1}\n"
"+\n"
"to double check what changed since the last round, and finally\n"
"+\n"
" $ git checkout -B @{-1}\n"
"+\n"
"to conclude (the last step is why a topic already in 'next' is\n"
"not replaced but updated incrementally).\n"
"+\n"
"Whether it is the initial round or a subsequent round, the topic\n"
"may not build even in isolation, or may break the build when\n"
"merged to integration branches due to bugs.  There may already\n"
"be obvious and trivial improvements suggested on the list.  The\n"
"maintainer often adds an extra commit, with \"SQUASH???\" in its\n"
"title, to fix things up, before publishing the integration\n"
"branches to make it usable by other developers for testing.\n"
"These changes are what the maintainer is not 100% committed to\n"
"(trivial typofixes etc. are often squashed directly into the\n"
"patches that need fixing, without being applied as a separate\n"
"\"SQUASH???\" commit), so that they can be removed easily as needed.\n"
"The expectation is that the original author will make corrections\n"
"in a reroll.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:350
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" *** By now, new topic branches are created and existing topic\n"
"     branches are updated.  The integration branches 'next', 'jch',\n"
"     and 'seen' need to be updated to contain them.\n"
" *** If there are topics that have been merged to 'master' and should\n"
"     be merged to 'maint', merge them to 'maint', and update the\n"
"     release notes to the next maintenance release.\n"
" *** Review the latest issue of \"What's cooking\" again.  Are topics\n"
"     that have been sufficiently long in 'next' ready to be merged to\n"
"     'master'?  Are topics we saw earlier and are in 'seen' now got\n"
"     positive reviews and are ready to be merged to 'next'?\n"
" *** If there are topics that have been cooking in 'next' long enough\n"
"     and should be merged to 'master', merge them to 'master', and\n"
"     update the release notes to the next feature release.\n"
" *** If there were patches directly made on 'maint', merge 'maint' to\n"
"     'master'; make sure that the result is what you want.\n"
"+\n"
" $ git checkout master\n"
" $ git merge -m \"Sync with 'maint'\" --no-log maint\n"
" $ git log -p --first-parent ORIG_HEAD..\n"
" $ make test\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:359
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" *** Prepare to update the 'jch' branch, which is used to represent\n"
"     somewhere between 'master' and 'seen' and often is slightly ahead\n"
"     of 'next', and the 'seen' branch, which is used to hold the rest.\n"
"+\n"
" $ Meta/Reintegrate master..jch >Meta/redo-jch.sh\n"
"+\n"
"The result is a script that lists topics to be merged in order to\n"
"rebuild the current 'jch'.  Do the same for 'seen'.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:431
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" *** Review the Meta/redo-jch.sh and Meta/redo-seen.sh scripts.  The\n"
"     former should have a line '### match next'---the idea is that\n"
"     merging the topics listed before the line on top of 'master'\n"
"     should result in a tree identical to that of 'next'.\n"
" *** As newly created topics are usually merged near the tip of\n"
"     'seen', add them to the end of the Meta/redo-seen.sh script.\n"
"     Among the topics that were in 'seen', there may be ones that\n"
"     are not quite ready for 'next' but are getting there.  Move\n"
"     them from Meta/redo-seen.sh to the end of Meta/redo-jch.sh.\n"
"     The expectation is that you'd use 'jch' as your daily driver\n"
"     as the first guinea pig, so you should choose carefully.\n"
" *** Now we are ready to start rebuilding 'jch' and merging topics to\n"
"     'next'.  For each branch whose tip is not merged to 'next', one\n"
"     of three things can happen:\n"
" **** The commits are all next-worthy; merge the topic to next;\n"
" **** The new parts are of mixed quality, but earlier ones are\n"
"      next-worthy; merge the early parts to next;\n"
" **** Nothing is next-worthy; do not do anything.\n"
"+\n"
"This step is aided with Meta/redo-jch.sh script created earlier.\n"
"If a topic that was already in 'next' gained a patch, the script\n"
"would list it as \"ai/topic~1\".  To include the new patch to the\n"
"updated 'next', drop the \"~1\" part; to keep it excluded, do not\n"
"touch the line.\n"
"+\n"
"If a topic that was not in 'next' should be merged to 'next', add\n"
"it before the '### match next' line.  Then:\n"
"+\n"
" $ git checkout --detach master\n"
" $ sh Meta/redo-jch.sh -c1\n"
"+\n"
"to rebuild the 'jch' branch from scratch.  \"-c1\" tells the script\n"
"to stop merging at the first line that begins with '###'\n"
"(i.e. the \"### match next\" line you added earlier).\n"
"+\n"
"At this point, build-test the result.  It may reveal semantic\n"
"conflicts (e.g. a topic renamed a variable, another added a new\n"
"reference to the variable under its old name), in which case\n"
"prepare an appropriate merge-fix first (see appendix), and\n"
"rebuild the 'jch' branch from scratch, starting at the tip of\n"
"'master', this time without using \"-c1\" to merge all topics.\n"
"+\n"
"Then do the same to 'next'.\n"
"+\n"
" $ git checkout next\n"
" $ sh Meta/redo-jch.sh -c1 -e\n"
"+\n"
"The \"-e\" option allows the merge message that comes from the\n"
"history of the topic and the comments in the \"What's cooking\" to\n"
"be edited.  The resulting tree should match 'jch^{/^### match next'}'\n"
"as the same set of topics are merged on 'master'; otherwise there\n"
"is a mismerge. Investigate why and do not proceed until the mismerge\n"
"is found and rectified.\n"
"+\n"
"If 'master' was updated before you started redoing 'next', then\n"
"+\n"
" $ git diff 'jch^{/^### match next}' next\n"
"+\n"
"would show differences that went into 'master' (which 'jch' has,\n"
"but 'next' does not yet---often it is updates to the release\n"
"notes).  Merge 'master' back to 'next' if that is the case.\n"
"+\n"
" $ git merge -m \"Sync with 'master'\" --no-log master\n"
"+\n"
"When all is well, clean up the redo-jch.sh script with\n"
"+\n"
" $ sh Meta/redo-jch.sh -u\n"
"+\n"
"This removes topics listed in the script that have already been\n"
"merged to 'master'.  This may lose '### match next' marker;\n"
"add it again to the appropriate place when it happens.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:444
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" *** Rebuild 'seen' on top of 'jch'.\n"
"+\n"
" $ git checkout -B seen jch\n"
" $ sh Meta/redo-seen.sh\n"
"+\n"
"When all is well, clean up the redo-seen.sh script with\n"
"+\n"
" $ sh Meta/redo-seen.sh -u\n"
"+\n"
"Double check by running\n"
"+\n"
" $ git branch --no-merged seen '??/*'\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:473
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" * Update \"What's cooking\" message to review the updates to\n"
"   existing topics, newly added topics and graduated topics.\n"
"+\n"
"This step is helped with Meta/cook script.\n"
"+\n"
" $ Meta/cook\n"
"+\n"
"This script inspects the history between master..seen, finds tips\n"
"of topic branches, compares what it found with the current\n"
"contents in Meta/whats-cooking.txt, and updates that file.\n"
"Topics not listed in the file but are found in master..seen are\n"
"added to the \"New topics\" section, topics listed in the file that\n"
"are no longer found in master..seen are moved to the \"Graduated to\n"
"master\" section, and topics whose commits changed their states\n"
"(e.g. used to be only in 'seen', now merged to 'next') are updated\n"
"with change markers \"<<\" and \">>\".\n"
"+\n"
"Look for lines enclosed in \"<<\" and \">>\"; they hold contents from\n"
"old file that are replaced by this integration round.  After\n"
"verifying them, remove the old part.  Review the description for\n"
"each topic and update its doneness and plan as needed.  To review\n"
"the updated plan, run\n"
"+\n"
"     $ Meta/cook -w\n"
"+\n"
"which will pick up comments given to the topics, such as \"Will\n"
"merge to 'next'\", etc. (see Meta/cook script to learn what kind\n"
"of phrases are supported).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:476
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" * Compile, test and install all four (five) integration branches;\n"
"   Meta/Dothem script may aid this step.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:479
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" * Format documentation if the 'master' branch was updated;\n"
"   Meta/dodoc.sh script may aid this step.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:482
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" * Push the integration branches out to public places; Meta/pushall\n"
"   script may aid this step.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:484
#, no-wrap
msgid "Observations\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:487
msgid "Some observations to be made."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:491
msgid ""
"Each topic is tested individually, and also together with other topics "
"cooking first in 'seen', then in 'jch' and then in 'next'.  Until it "
"matures, no part of it is merged to 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:496
msgid ""
"A topic already in 'next' can get fixes while still in 'next'.  Such a topic "
"will have many merges to 'next' (in other words, \"git log --first-parent "
"next\" will show many \"Merge branch 'ai/topic' to next\" for the same "
"topic."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:500
msgid ""
"An unobvious fix for 'maint' is cooked in 'next' and then merged to 'master' "
"to make extra sure it is Ok and then merged to 'maint'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:505
msgid ""
"Even when 'next' becomes empty (in other words, all topics prove stable and "
"are merged to 'master' and \"git diff master next\" shows empty), it has "
"tons of merge commits that will never be in 'master'."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:509
msgid ""
"In principle, \"git log --first-parent master..next\" should show nothing "
"but merges (in practice, there are fixup commits and reverts that are not "
"merges)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:513
msgid ""
"Commits near the tip of a topic branch that are not in 'next' are fair game "
"to be discarded, replaced or rewritten.  Commits already merged to 'next' "
"will not be."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:517
msgid ""
"Being in the 'next' branch is not a guarantee for a topic to be included in "
"the next feature release.  Being in the 'master' branch typically is."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:525
msgid ""
"Due to the nature of \"SQUASH???\" fix-ups, if the original author agrees "
"with the suggested changes, it is OK to squash them to appropriate patches "
"in the next round (when the suggested change is small enough, the author "
"should not even bother with \"Helped-by\").  It is also OK to drop them from "
"the next round when the original author does not agree with the suggestion, "
"but the author is expected to say why somewhere in the discussion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:528
#, no-wrap
msgid "Appendix"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ~
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:531
#, no-wrap
msgid "Preparing a \"merge-fix\""
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:539
msgid ""
"A merge of two topics may not textually conflict but still have conflict at "
"the semantic level. A classic example is for one topic to rename a variable "
"and all its uses, while another topic adds a new use of the variable under "
"its old name. When these two topics are merged together, the reference to "
"the variable newly added by the latter topic will still use the old name in "
"the result."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:545
msgid ""
"The Meta/Reintegrate script that is used by redo-jch and redo-seen scripts "
"implements a crude but usable way to work around this issue.  When the "
"script merges branch $X, it checks if \"refs/merge-fix/$X\" exists, and if "
"so, the effect of it is squashed into the result of the mechanical merge.  "
"In other words,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:547
#, no-wrap
msgid "     $ echo $X | Meta/Reintegrate\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:549
msgid "is roughly equivalent to this sequence:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:554
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"     $ git merge --rerere-autoupdate $X\n"
"     $ git commit\n"
"     $ git cherry-pick -n refs/merge-fix/$X\n"
"     $ git commit --amend\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:558
msgid ""
"The goal of this \"prepare a merge-fix\" step is to come up with a commit "
"that can be squashed into a result of mechanical merge to correct semantic "
"conflicts."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:564
msgid ""
"After finding that the result of merging branch \"ai/topic\" to an "
"integration branch had such a semantic conflict, say seen~4, check the "
"problematic merge out on a detached HEAD, edit the working tree to fix the "
"semantic conflict, and make a separate commit to record the fix-up:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:570
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"     $ git checkout seen~4\n"
"     $ git show -s --pretty=%s ;# double check\n"
"     Merge branch 'ai/topic' to seen\n"
"     $ edit\n"
"     $ git commit -m 'merge-fix/ai/topic' -a\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:573
msgid "Then make a reference \"refs/merge-fix/ai/topic\" to point at this result:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:575
#, no-wrap
msgid "     $ git update-ref refs/merge-fix/ai/topic HEAD\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:578
msgid "Then double check the result by asking Meta/Reintegrate to redo the merge:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:582
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"     $ git checkout seen~5 ;# the parent of the problem merge\n"
"     $ echo ai/topic | Meta/Reintegrate\n"
"     $ git diff seen~4\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:586
msgid ""
"This time, because you prepared refs/merge-fix/ai/topic, the resulting merge "
"should have been tweaked to include the fix for the semantic conflict."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:598
msgid ""
"Note that this assumes that the order in which conflicting branches are "
"merged does not change.  If the reason why merging ai/topic branch needs "
"this merge-fix is because another branch merged earlier to the integration "
"branch changed the underlying assumption ai/topic branch made (e.g. ai/topic "
"branch added a site to refer to a variable, while the other branch renamed "
"that variable and adjusted existing use sites), and if you changed redo-jch "
"(or redo-seen) script to merge ai/topic branch before the other branch, then "
"the above merge-fix should not be applied while merging ai/topic, but should "
"instead be applied while merging the other branch.  You would need to move "
"the fix to apply to the other branch, perhaps like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/howto/maintain-git.txt:601
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      $ mf=refs/merge-fix\n"
"      $ git update-ref $mf/$the_other_branch $mf/ai/topic\n"
"      $ git update-ref -d $mf/ai/topic\n"
msgstr ""
